{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787124961,"nonce":"t01d0iffEdahwsbkFpiPHex6pn4=","title":"**The demonstration.** The owner ruled on 2026-08-19 that `unacked_reminder` is dropped. No ticket recorded it, so this is that ticket, and it exists because the surface is roughly four times what the ruling sounds like. Measured, not estimated: `git grep -lI 'unacked_reminder\\|UnackedReminder\\|unackedReminder'` returns **95 files**. It is not one flag. It is a subsystem with five parts.","message":"1. **A periodic job on a one-minute cadence.** `internal/platform/jobs/kinds.go:63` (`KindNotifyUnackedReminder = \"notify.unacked_reminder\"`), its args at `internal/platform/jobs/args.go:229-238`, its handler slot at `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:141,224`, and its schedule at `registry.go:304` — `add(time.Minute, …)`. Plus the worker itself, `internal/notification/worker/worker.go`, and the sweep query `internal/notification/repository/reminders.go`.\n2. **A Reason with a subject allocation.** `ReasonUnackedReminder` at `internal/notification/domain/reason.go:78`, in the enum list at `:117`, allocated `SubjectAlertGroup` at `:241`. It is the eighteenth value of an enum whose ceiling is asserted in three places — `notifications_reason_ck`, `policies_reasons_ck`, and `domain.MaxPolicyReasons` — exactly the trio migration `00060` moved from 19 to 18 when it deleted `storm`. That migration is the template for this one.\n3. **FOUR org settings keys, not one.** `internal/identity/domain/settings.go:47` `unacked_reminder_after_s`, `:53` `unacked_reminder_mention`, `:55` `unacked_reminder_mention_list`, `:59` `unacked_reminder_mention_min_severity`. Plus the bound at `:248`, the four resolved fields at `:301-323`, the accessor at `:350`, `internal/identity/domain/mention.go`, the declarative config, and `deploy/helm/oto/values.yaml`.\n4. **A Slack render path with checked-in goldens.** `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go`, `renderer.go`, `testdata/reply_unacked_reminder_mention.golden.json`, `test/fixtures/slack/broadcast_unacked_reminder.message.json` and its `index.json` entry.\n5. **A SCOPE-BOUNDARY gate that exists to stop this concept moving.** `test/scope/scalar_reminder_test.go` is AC-52 (SPEC §P-19c): four AST instruments proving `unacked_reminder_after_s` is a scalar `*int` in every layer. Its header states the rule it enforces — \"ONE STAGE, FOREVER\" — and a grep deliberately cannot do its job.\n\n**Why it matters.** Two reasons, and the second is why this is worth doing rather than merely worth recording.\n\nThe reminder is the **surviving reshape of the escalation feature**, not an incidental extra. `docs/design/SCOPE-BOUNDARY.md:185` is the door decision: `escalation` was RESHAPED, not cut — the reason renamed to `unacked_reminder`, the worker renamed `escalation.check` → `notify.unacked_reminder`, `escalate_after_s` → `unacked_reminder_after_s`, individual `\u003c@U…\u003e` mentions dropped from `mention_on_reminder` (`:186`), and a binding clause added to §G.9 fixing oto at one stage forever. Dropping the reminder now retires the last of the escalation surface, and it means the reshape argument at `:185`, `:266` and `:384` describes a mechanism that no longer exists. Those lines are BINDING text. They do not get quietly deleted; they get a recorded supersession, or the boundary document starts lying the way `00011:197` did in `e5c060b`.\n\nSecond, it **dissolves the first hazard blocking `7570090` stage 4**. `internal/notification/repository/reminders.go:114-127` keys the once-per-generation latch on the literal `(alert_group, group_id)` pair, and its own comment says so: \"⚠️ MOVING unacked_reminder TO ANOTHER SUBJECT WOULD SILENTLY UNLATCH IT. Nothing would fail: this clause would simply stop matching the row it wrote, every sweep would find the group again, and one reminder stage (§G.9.1) would become a nag every sweep interval — the ladder oto refuses, built by accident.\" Stage 4 deletes `alert_groups`, which is exactly that move. Dropping the reminder makes the hazard disappear instead of needing a new key for it. **That is the ordering constraint: this lands before stage 4, not after.**\n\n**Where.** The five parts above, with their file:line. Plus the enum-ceiling trio (`notifications_reason_ck`, `policies_reasons_ck`, `domain.MaxPolicyReasons`, and the contract's `maxItems` held to the same number by `test/contract/dto_schema_test.go`), a forward migration in `00060`'s shape, `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` (the reason enum, the three `unacked_reminder_after_seconds` sites and their `maxItems`), the regenerated TS client and valibot validators, `web/src/features/notifications/PoliciesSection.tsx`, `web/src/features/notifications/vocabulary.ts`, `web/src/features/settings/TuningSection.tsx` and `tuningCopy.ts`, `internal/platform/config/tuning.go`, `internal/platform/validate/validate.go`, `tools/lintvocab/baseline.txt`, and SPEC §G.9.1, ADR 0013, ADR 0020, `CONTEXT.md`.\n\n⚠️ **Two things are NOT decided, and neither is mine to decide.** Both were found by reading the surface, not by running anything, and either answer makes the rest mechanical.\n\n1. **Does `mention_on_reminder` go with it?** `SCOPE-BOUNDARY §5.2` restricts it to usergroups and `!here`/`!channel`, and calls that restriction \"the ruling\" (`:384`). Three of the four settings keys are the mention subsystem, so this is most of the settings surface. If the only thing that mentions is the reminder, they go together. If a mention is a property of any Slack delivery, they stay and only the reminder trigger goes. **Not determined — nothing was run.**\n2. **What happens to the §G.9 binding clause and the `escalation` banned word?** The clause says a second stage is permanently out. With no stage at all, does the clause become \"there are no reminder stages and never will be\", or does it retire along with the mechanism? The banned word almost certainly stays — the reason it was banned is unrelated to whether oto reminds — but that should be said, not assumed.\n\n**Scope.** Large. Ninety-five files, a periodic job, a database enum narrowing with three ceilings, four settings keys, checked-in Slack goldens, and a SCOPE-BOUNDARY gate whose whole purpose is to make this concept hard to move. The enum narrowing has a good template in `00060`; the boundary-document supersession does not, and is the part that needs a person.\n\n**Done when.** The `notify.unacked_reminder` job kind, its schedule, its worker and its sweep query are gone; `ReasonUnackedReminder` has left the enum, the two CHECKs and `MaxPolicyReasons`, with a forward migration in `00060`'s shape and Down assertions added to `TestEveryMigrationDownTo00028IsReversible`; the settings keys named by the answer to question 1 are gone from `settings.go`, the declarative config, the Helm values and the tuning screen; the contract, the regenerated client and the validators no longer carry `unacked_reminder_after_seconds`; `test/scope/scalar_reminder_test.go` is deleted rather than weakened, because a gate over a field that does not exist is worse than no gate; and SPEC §G.9.1 plus `SCOPE-BOUNDARY.md:185,266,384` record the supersession rather than being edited to pretend the reshape never happened. What must not persist is a settings key an operator can still write, or a boundary document describing a mechanism that was deleted — the first is the defect `0457f1f`, `35d4248`, `39e48e2` and `27a1860` all closed, and the second is `e5c060b`.","files":null}]}